#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134695} :activity/id #uuid "93a7b58c-f55f-5517-ad99-318bb8790e55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284 :topic/id #uuid "90a1bf39-5b4c-5a6f-82d9-c3528eb17b2c" :topic/math-academy-id 2166} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135059 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is easy to show that the system is inconsistent (i.e., it has no solution). However, in real-world problems, we might want to find an approximate solution to the system. \n\nLet's formulate the problem a little more rigorously. Firstly, we write down the system in the form $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/22/22b8abd4638e579875755f482442cf1dbf89653bd9d396b4a802217e25177839.svg)\n\nWe are interested in vectors $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ such that $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ is very close to $\\mathbf b.$ More precisely, we wish to *minimize the distance* between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b.$ Recall that the distance between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nThe smaller the value of $\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert,$ the better our approximation $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ will be. \n\nIn terms of vector subspaces, we wish to find a vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and is closest to $\\mathbf{b}.$\nTherefore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ must be the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nIt can be shown that ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is found by solving the corresponding **normal equation**, given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this at the end of the lesson. Note that the normal equation is obtained by left-multiplying the equation $A {\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = \\mathbf b$ by $A^T.$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = (A^T \\! A)^{-1}A^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{99}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{33} \\\\ -\\frac{38}{33}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is called the **least-squares solution** of the given system.\n\n**Watch out!** We assumed that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, which guarantees that $A^T \\! A\\,$ is invertible. However, if the columns of $A$ are linearly *dependent,* then we have to use a different approach to solve the problem." :tutorial/id #uuid "548ecdec-2401-51e1-b2cf-d63f0b5a595d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3636 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3719452-213a-5a8f-92fa-ac5912c1a8ba" :step/math-academy-id 20269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :question/id #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" :question/math-academy-id "e-5588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482611}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "eb5011df-f314-5469-89c6-7bc2e1633d5b" :step/math-academy-id 20270 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133609 :question/id #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482605}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "bd48fc68-e028-5256-934f-98b803b1f3f4" :step/math-academy-id 20271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133663 :question/id #uuid "af2b3f92-fed5-5a50-b662-eaadd9b7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048506}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500322}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "e9d074bb-35df-566a-80ad-6c15d1e9fe5f" :step/math-academy-id 27532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135146 :tutorial/content "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/67/677276828b5438bcf8d73facec02bc738e2e4f02bb6c5ff83bd5ae41902dd8ea.svg) matrix with linearly independent columns. We'll now explain why the least-squares solution ![](images/a4/a4d10ece37c4369a97f57734306d77d35e94a240b68f2625b4d45791bdc13378.svg) to the least-squares problem \n\n![](images/f7/f72b4cb4f9fd00c6a409ed62ae97aad9915179065d14609810459cbd7b5b63c2.svg)\n\ncan be found by solving the normal equation\n![](images/dc/dcd7a5cf3452038ee326ed1b4a5ab2913f41ad408849ddda132cbdfee7c35887.svg)\n\nThe vector ![](images/eb/eba4e0fbcf4207b493c3a3a4eecc12df848d9d995708881cce8542ae5d04bdd9.svg) must be the orthogonal projection of ![](images/63/639196a1a30d6f6280e3cf491e6cf3c2b0b991892970f7b641a9b300defb034f.svg) onto the subspace spanned by the columns of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg)\n\n![](images/02/024c12b780df0414ab1cf012e5733603fb1167c9533e199c46628d73ad12ebd9.png)\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n![](images/c3/c3f058a925aef4913b39914985d02cfb3d7b2752c0116aa43b2e73d804fa29d4.svg) \n\nwhich is equivalent to saying that ![](images/c5/c54ecdc03925384b80bdbddc9df105fb34d4b31426570aa7c6446815c6a90ed9.svg) is orthogonal to each column ![](images/16/162e4a7398d3a8c47cc967b78bf43167256270c609bfdc271e46a2ea32ade145.svg) of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Thus, the dot product of the columns of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and the vector ![](images/c5/c54ecdc03925384b80bdbddc9df105fb34d4b31426570aa7c6446815c6a90ed9.svg) must be equal to ![](images/37/37dd4bc8064071b5d4219d47ac24847861bdbe0c82b48e4997c5d7d8b4a3d566.svg)\n\n![](images/57/5701e3d1325d0ec137b7d405796114e3044f5076c11fa9e2e10fe70d16919a88.svg)\n\nThis system of equations can be written in terms of matrix products as\n\n![](images/92/921a6627dff46463fc16fc80fe16353fb80942f08c5c632d6665920be96676ed.svg) \n\nFinally, expanding the left-hand side, we obtain our normal equation:\n\n![](images/86/867c103511eeb1e208028ea1a3af3eb4b397c50a3131efea683b55d959d4ec4e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "43d14ff9-b350-5406-9f4b-64096d8f3215" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6154 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Normal Equation"} :step/id #uuid "df64bb85-56ef-5ee5-80d4-956e1d949a18" :step/math-academy-id 37337}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (Without Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134026}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500387} :activity/id #uuid "4afc5eb0-b00c-5311-ad64-1f529d40ac87" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284 :topic/id #uuid "90a1bf39-5b4c-5a6f-82d9-c3528eb17b2c" :topic/math-academy-id 2166} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125e5ea0-2d09-53c7-bf5a-6db86f01788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324136} {:answer/id #uuid "16df845b-8aca-58e3-ae92-9d86f331bce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324173} {:answer/id #uuid "24d2ae87-e1f9-50af-9aa6-98e8929a2d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{20} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324274} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4ad4a-f6d5-57ac-9b52-100c6666021e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325558} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ec109-3e58-5c22-b7fa-223132d14aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325558} :answer-field/id #uuid "24dd5431-2243-5962-ae01-cbb9b9be0bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "082f22b9-7dbc-5c7c-88d0-775a3d957e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-41776" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0c175108-3251-5f6f-a837-933e8a149a06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c9ab58-bd09-5df1-8d1d-2d4ce831e5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499698} {:answer/id #uuid "4fda416c-29ab-5041-9567-21fe9188edc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499807} {:answer/id #uuid "6123a26d-2828-5a47-a292-99e6c3705513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499845} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbbd696-cd17-5e43-a03c-c815bdcc9a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500039} {:answer/id #uuid "c898ab35-05fd-5fa1-8c7c-3ba6a9711cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499845} :answer-field/id #uuid "112e9326-3d57-5ecf-afb8-b6f921f35c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7b37b44d-e7cb-5e9e-b8af-e9f1e4941b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-140953" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1daadfd6-2782-5d18-911b-b764bf042f15"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59881df0-2ac6-532e-8fb9-91b698aa746f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352356} {:answer/id #uuid "6136a078-6ec6-579a-809a-d2077533b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352433} {:answer/id #uuid "76500562-20f2-5c7e-bdb3-4992adaf7d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352607} {:answer/id #uuid "7917c258-2cdc-5cb9-a21d-ebdf986ccad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{101}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352623} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29578-9f41-5fba-9274-bad7f34afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352661} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e227d03-ecbf-552f-86b6-2f39084c0aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "22b88813-fc85-5da7-b050-31934e04cdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85284" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{110}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a561b4d8-a24f-5ea9-9176-71cdc5599a2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f733f4-12f3-5c07-8f4b-304b44459b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352230} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c331c-8cc2-5e5e-b4b6-de1a54f34fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352806} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ad2f12-4695-596d-8cec-b7f38fb28a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353297} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a56cfe-ee7f-5cca-9b65-eb74bde205f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353390} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c8975d-a61b-537d-897c-5fdec1b16da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353390} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda82a11-1d36-50fa-8ecc-f1dd1a7dc6ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48fc2839-660f-57b5-b91c-b2fcd5bae103" :question/math-academy-id "q-85899" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d22a3088-3980-554b-950c-e1334497ac66" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc9412d-db1d-5f0b-bf46-1547fc9bc9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382561} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab9fcd-0d01-5a7c-a562-b332d1fa753b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a397f58-508e-5d63-aeae-854fad3a6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} {:answer/id #uuid "2756ce9d-d692-57b4-88bb-5f748720577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382564} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b1550-1387-5406-a483-f580bc72d844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cb7faf-1c5d-5786-81e1-1833c5631b56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbf886ad-eced-5102-99df-0e48cd09970a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85905" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "efc7b297-2494-521d-8ca9-fb8275cf05d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500391}}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (Without Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499645}]}
